WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.414
You are looking after the coats at a friend's wedding.

2
00:00:05.414 --> 00:00:09.988
Each coat goes on a hook, and you write the ticket number next to the hook in a small book.

3
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.855
Halfway through the evening, the book falls into a bowl of punch.

4
00:00:14.067 --> 00:00:17.686
The coats are all still there, safe on their hooks.

5
00:00:17.686 --> 00:00:20.028
Then a guest hands you ticket 41.

6
00:00:20.028 --> 00:00:21.376
Which coat is hers?

7
00:00:21.376 --> 00:00:21.944
No idea.

8
00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:25.025
Terraform has the same three things.

9
00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:26.845
Your code says what you want.

10
00:00:26.845 --> 00:00:31.363
The real world is the containers Docker is running: those are the coats.

11
00:00:31.567 --> 00:00:36.864
And in between is the book: the state, a file called terraform.tfstate.

12
00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:40.219
Its main job is one line per object.

13
00:00:40.219 --> 00:00:46.350
This address, docker_container.web, is the container with ID d8eb279c.

14
00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:47.839
Ticket, and hook.

15
00:00:48.067 --> 00:00:50.415
Every plan begins with a refresh.

16
00:00:50.415 --> 00:00:57.035
Terraform asks the provider, the plugin that talks to Docker, about every object in the book.

17
00:00:57.035 --> 00:00:58.743
Is d8eb279c still there?

18
00:00:58.743 --> 00:01:01.661
Then it compares the book with your code.

19
00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:05.617
Which raises the question you are probably already thinking.

20
00:01:05.617 --> 00:01:07.618
Docker can list every container.

21
00:01:07.618 --> 00:01:09.993
Why does Terraform need a book at all?

22
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.882
Because Docker cannot tell which containers this folder owns.

23
00:01:13.882 --> 00:01:15.511
Some might be a teammate's.

24
00:01:15.511 --> 00:01:18.287
Some might belong to another Terraform folder.

25
00:01:18.287 --> 00:01:20.701
Only the book says which coats are ours.

26
00:01:21.333 --> 00:01:23.672
So let's drop the book in the punch.

27
00:01:23.672 --> 00:01:26.270
You delete terraform.tfstate by mistake.

28
00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:28.089
The container keeps running.

29
00:01:28.089 --> 00:01:30.363
What does the next apply try to do?

30
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.872
It tries to create everything again, as if new.

31
00:01:35.872 --> 00:01:39.417
The code asks for a container, and the book knows of none.

32
00:01:39.417 --> 00:01:42.961
With Docker that fails, because the name is already taken.

33
00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:47.913
In a cloud, where names don't clash, you can end up with two of everything.

34
00:01:47.913 --> 00:01:50.001
The coats were never the problem.

35
00:01:50.001 --> 00:01:50.824
The book was.

36
00:01:51.633 --> 00:01:52.878
Now a smaller slip.

37
00:01:52.878 --> 00:01:57.727
You rename the resource label from web to site, because site reads better.

38
00:01:57.727 --> 00:01:59.365
In git it looks harmless.

39
00:01:59.365 --> 00:02:01.068
Same container, same name.

40
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.779
What does the plan say?

41
00:02:05.433 --> 00:02:07.336
Terraform reads it differently.

42
00:02:07.336 --> 00:02:12.736
The book has a line for web, and the code has no web any more, so web will be destroyed.

43
00:02:12.736 --> 00:02:16.909
The code has a site, and the book has none, so site will be created.

44
00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:24.306
That is the cloakroom attendant who sees a new ticket number, throws the coat in the bin, and buys an identical one.

45
00:02:24.306 --> 00:02:29.563
The real container would be rebuilt, data and all, to change a name only you can see.

46
00:02:29.767 --> 00:02:33.063
The fix is one block in your code: a moved block.

47
00:02:33.063 --> 00:02:36.561
From docker_container.web, to docker_container.site.

48
00:02:36.561 --> 00:02:40.261
It tells Terraform the object only changed its address.

49
00:02:40.467 --> 00:02:41.279
Plan again.

50
00:02:41.279 --> 00:02:45.412
docker_container.web has moved to docker_container.site.

51
00:02:45.412 --> 00:02:47.552
Zero to add, zero to destroy.

52
00:02:47.552 --> 00:02:51.907
Apply, and the book gets the new address, with the same ID.

53
00:02:52.133 --> 00:02:53.541
So the rule is short.

54
00:02:53.541 --> 00:02:56.424
A rename in code is a destroy to Terraform.

55
00:02:56.424 --> 00:03:00.111
Add a moved block, and check the plan for has moved to.

56
00:03:00.833 --> 00:03:03.981
One place where the cloakroom picture stops short.

57
00:03:03.981 --> 00:03:06.688
A cloakroom book notes a ticket and a hook.

58
00:03:06.688 --> 00:03:11.157
Terraform's state copies every detail of each object, secrets included.

59
00:03:11.367 --> 00:03:16.427
In a test, a variable marked sensitive printed as sensitive value in the plan.

60
00:03:16.427 --> 00:03:18.828
The state file held it in plain text.

61
00:03:18.828 --> 00:03:22.267
Anyone who can read your state can read your secrets.

62
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.954
So treat the book like a password file.

63
00:03:24.954 --> 00:03:28.101
Never commit it to git, and never edit it by hand.

64
00:03:28.101 --> 00:03:32.632
Read it through commands, terraform state list and terraform state show.

65
00:03:32.833 --> 00:03:40.093
And in a team, keep one copy on a remote backend that locks it, so only one run at a time can write in the book.

66
00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:42.173
The moral of the story.

67
00:03:42.173 --> 00:03:45.933
The containers are easy to see, so they feel like what matters.

68
00:03:45.933 --> 00:03:49.575
But the state is what tells Terraform which ones are its own.

69
00:03:49.575 --> 00:03:51.664
Lose the book, and it builds again.

70
00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:54.231
Rename without telling it, and it rebuilds.

71
00:03:54.833 --> 00:03:55.843
A quick check.

72
00:03:55.843 --> 00:04:00.242
You run terraform state rm on the site container, then apply.

73
00:04:00.242 --> 00:04:02.477
The container is still running.

74
00:04:02.477 --> 00:04:03.415
What happens?

75
00:04:06.067 --> 00:04:09.125
state rm only crossed the line out of the book.

76
00:04:09.125 --> 00:04:16.021
The code still asks for the container, so apply tries to create it, and Docker refuses the duplicate name.
